r columns, so the result has duplicate names. Many drivers silently keep only one of each. It also pulls columns you likely don't need, such as user PII. List the columns you want, for example `o.id, o.total, o.created_at, u.email`.\n\n2. **`lower(u.email) = ...` can't use a plain index on `email`.** That forces a scan of `users`. You have three fixes:\n   - Add an expression index: `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`\n   - Use a case-insensitive type, such as `citext` in Postgres.\n   - Store emails normalized to lowercase and compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'` directly. Lowercase the input in the application.\n\n3. **Hardcoded literal.** If this runs from application code, use a bind parameter (`$1` or `?`) to avoid injection and allow plan reuse.\n\n4. **Index on `orders.user_id`.** Make sure one exists, since the join depends on it.\n\n5. **No `ORDER BY` or `LIMIT`.** If a user can have many orders, add an explicit order (for example `ORDER BY o.created_at DESC`) and a limit if you're paginating.\n\nA revised version, assuming Postgres:\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.total, o.created_at, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = lower($1)\nORDER BY o.created_at DESC;\n```\n\nThis assumes the expression index from point 2. If you tell me the database and the table definitions, I can tailor the column list and indexes.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}